<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=fishing-net">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fishing net</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 8300 BCE</span> · Also known as Fish net</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A fishing net is a net used to catch fish, usually a mesh of knotted thread that serves as an improvised trap. The oldest known fishing net was found in 1913 at Antrea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, made of willow and dated to 8300 BCE. Fishing net sinkers from 27,000 BCE have been found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, the oldest fishing implements known. Nets appear in Egyptian tomb paintings from 3000 BCE, and Roman writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> refer to them. Early nets were woven from grasses, vines and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flax">flax</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, and the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nylon">nylon</a> nets emerged in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1949.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fishing-net.jpg" alt="Fishing net" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Church · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_fishing_net_in_Brandon_Creek_-_geograph.org.uk_-_921094.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cloth and textile technology mattered greatly, from clothing and bags to <b>fishing nets</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/caral-supe-civilisation">Caral–Supe civilisation · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Men made <b>fishing nets</b> and ropes of cotton and palm and hollowed dugout canoes, kanoa, that held from 2 to 150 people, an average one 15–20.</blockquote><a href="/culture/taino">Taíno · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>Cotton <b>fishing nets</b> were a key product.</blockquote><a href="/state/caral-supe">Caral-Supe</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 8300 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Antrea, Karelia, Finland</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Net sinkers from 27,000 BCE found in Korea are the oldest fishing implements known, but they are weights, not a net.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/rope">Rope<span>c. 24,000 years ago</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=fishing-net">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
